{"name":"Last Warning","hex":"#d30f3f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"last","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Final, ultimate, coming after all others of its kind.","Most recent, latest, last so far."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["The (one) immediately before the present.","Closest in the past, or closest but one if the closest was very recent; of days, sometimes thought to specifically refer to the instance closest to seven days (one week) ago, or the most recent instance before seven days (one week) ago."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/last"},{"term":"warning","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle of warn"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An instance of warning someone.","Something spoken or written that is intended to warn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warning"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Last Warning (1928 film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Last Warning is a 1928 sound part-talkie American mystery film directed by Paul Leni, and starring Laura La Plante, Montagu Love, and Margaret Livingston. In addition to sequences with audible dialogue or talking sequences, the film features a synchronized musical score and sound effects along with English intertitles. The film apparently only survives in a cut-down edited silent version which was made for theaters that had not yet converted to sound. The soundtrack for the sound version, which was also released on sound-on-disc format, survives in private hands on Vitaphone type discs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/The_Last_Warning_%281929%29_poster.jpg/330px-The_Last_Warning_%281929%29_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Warning_(1928_film)","wikidata":"Q3837985"},{"title":"The Last Warning (1938 film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Last Warning is a 1938 American mystery film directed by Albert S. Rogell and written by Edmund Hartmann. It is based on the 1938 novel The Dead Don't Care by Jonathan Latimer. The film stars Preston Foster, Frank Jenks, Kay Linaker, E. E. Clive, Joyce Compton and Frances Robinson. The film was released on January 6, 1939, by Universal Pictures."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/69/The_Last_Warning_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Warning_(1938_film)","wikidata":"Q65062393"},{"title":"Last Warning (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Last Warning is a compilation album by New York hardcore band Agnostic Front. It was released in 1993 on Relativity Records, marketed by Roadrunner Records, and follows 1992's studio album, One Voice."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ab/Lastwarning.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_Warning_(album)","wikidata":"Q3285159"},{"title":"Last","blocks":[{"text":"A last is a mechanical form used by shoemakers and cordwainers in the manufacture and repair of shoes. Lasts come in many styles and sizes, depending on the exact job they are designed for. Common variations include simple one-size lasts used for repairing soles and heels, custom-purpose mechanized lasts used in modern mass production, and custom-made lasts used in the making of bespoke footwear. Lasts are made of firm materials—hardwoods, cast iron, and high-density plastics—to withstand contact with wetted leather and the strong forces involved in reshaping it. Since the early 19th century, lasts typically come in pairs to match the separate shapes of the right and left feet. The development of an automated lasting machine by the Surinamese-American Jan Ernst Matzeliger in the 1880s was a major development in shoe production, immediately improving quality, halving prices, and eliminating the previous putting-out systems surrounding shoemaking centers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG/330px-PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last","wikidata":"Q1069931"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wooden lasts in a shoemaker's workshop in Hamburg, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jan Ernst Matzeliger in 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_ernst_matzeliger.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Matzeliger's lasting machine, complex enough the patent office had to send an agent to Lynn to see it in operation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZwikmachine_1885.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/1280px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The personal lasts of Audrey Hepburn and Ava Gardner at the Ferragamo Museum in Florence, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg/330px-Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg/960px-Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg/330px-Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Shoemakers_metal_last_on_stand_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg/250px-Shoemakers_metal_last_on_stand_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg